🧱 The Shape and Dimensions of a Tensor
   You can understand tensors by looking at their "Rank" (the number of axes or dimensions they have):
Rank / Dim      Math Name              What it Represents in ML 			                                    Visual rep
------------|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------
Rank 0      |    Scalar      | single number (e.g., a loss score or learning rate)                                       |         47
Rank 1      |    Vector      | 1D list of numbers (e.g., a single 768-dimension Ollama embedding)                        | [0.1, -0.4, 0.8]
Rank 2      |    Matrix      | 2D grid of numbers with rows and columns e.g., a single table chunk                       | [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
Rank 3      |    3D Tensor   | cube of numbers stacked together e.g colored image with Height, Width, & 3 Color Channels.| A stack of matrices
Rank 4+     |    3D+ Tensor  | Complex data shapes (e.g., a Batch of colored video clips with dimensions:                | Hyper-dim space
            |                | [BatchSize, Frames, Height, Width, Channels]).                                            | 
            |                |                                                                                           |
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


A tensor is structurally identical to a NumPy array, but it includes two specialized upgrades designed specifically for AI:
  Hardware Acceleration: NumPy arrays are strictly locked to run on your computer's CPU. Tensors (in frameworks like PyTorch or TensorFlow) 
                         can live directly inside your GPU's VRAM, making mathematical multiplications up to 100× faster.
  Automatic Differentiation (Autograd): Tensors track their own mathematical history. When you pass a tensor through a neural network, it 
                        remembers every operation performed on it. This allows the system to instantly run calculus backward (backpropagation) 
                        to calculate gradients and update model weights during training.

  when you fetch a vector embedding from Ollama, you are receiving a Rank 1 Tensor (a 768-element list).


stores plugin
	│
	├── How embeddings/vectors are stored
	├── Vector store
	├── Document store
	├── Index store
	├── Flat index
	├── IVF
	├── PQ
	├── SQ
	└── Other indexing structures

Store/index = how do we organize vectors for efficient searching?

High-dimensional space: 
 An N-dimensional mathematical space where N is large. An embedding with N numerical components can be viewed as a point/vector in 
 an N-dimensional space.


Not all Indexing types support all search types.

Vector Indexing methods:
-----------------------
  1⃣️ FLAT
  2⃣️ IVF
  3⃣️ HNSV
  4⃣️ SPARSED-INVERTED-INDEX
  5⃣️ GRAPH INDEXING
  6⃣️ QUANTIZATION INDEXING
  7⃣️ HASHING

0⃣️ FLAT:
1⃣️ IVF:
  IVF speeds up vector search by dividing vectors into clusters. During a search, the query is compared first with the cluster 
  representatives/centroid, and only the vectors inside the most relevant clusters are searched. Then rank based on representatives/
  centroid compare. Pick top K then compare query vector with each vector in this top K.
  Ex:
  	Query
  	↓
  	V1 V2 V3 V4 V5 V6 V7 V8 V9 V10 V11 V12
  	IVF first groups them into clusters:
                  All vectors
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ▼          ▼          ▼

       Cluster A   Cluster B   Cluster C

       V1 V3 V7     V2 V5      V4 V6 V8 V9 V10 V11 V12

          ●           ●           ●
      Centroid A   Centroid B   Centroid C

  	Query arrives:
   		Query vector
      		│
      		▼
		Compare with cluster centroids

       		Query
        	│
   	   ┌────┼────┐
   	   ▼    ▼    ▼

   	   ●    ●    ●
   	   A    B    C

   	distance
    		0.9  0.2  1.4
  		Cluster B is closest.
  	So instead of searching all 12 vectors:
    		V1 V2 V3 V4 V5 V6 V7 V8 V9 V10 V11 V12
  	we search only:
    		Cluster B
		V2
		V5

  	Then calculate the actual similarity against those vectors and return the closest ones.

  'inverted index' means, is maintaining a doc list for a given word, listing all doc's that contain that word.
   when searching docs for a given word, only need to look-up related doc's from this list.

  IVF (Inverted File Index) clusters vectors and searches only the clusters closest to the query, rather than comparing the query against 
  every vector. The number of clusters searched controls the speed-versus-accuracy trade-off.
2⃣️ QUANTIZATION INDEXING
  1⃣️ scalar quantization (SQ) - Experimental coding Difficulty rating: 3–4/10
    Each floating-point value in a vector is independently converted to a smaller integer representation. 
    This reduces storage and can speed up vector comparison, but is lossy because the original floating-point values cannot be recovered 
    exactly.
    ex:
    original vector:
    	A = [-0.82, -0.31, 0.14, 0.67, 0.91]
    	B = [-0.75, -0.28, 0.10, 0.70, 0.88]
    becomes after quantization
    	A → [23, 88, 145, 213, 244]
    	B → [32, 92, 140, 217, 240]
    The vector database can then perform its comparison using the quantized representations rather than the original float values:
    Original comparison:
    	[-0.82, -0.31, 0.14, 0.67, 0.91]
    	[-0.75, -0.28, 0.10, 0.70, 0.88]
    versus quantized comparison:
    	[23, 88, 145, 213, 244]
    	[32, 92, 140, 217, 240]
    Query vector
     	↓
    	Quantize using same quantization scheme
     	↓
    	[24, 89, 146, 214, 243]
    	Stored vector compare
     	↓
    	[23, 88, 145, 213, 244]
  2⃣️ Product quantization (PQ) - Experimental coding Difficulty rating: 5–6/10
    PQ compresses vectors by splitting each vector into sub-vectors and representing each sub-vector by the ID of its closest codebook 
    entry (codeword). codewords. codebook. A codebook contains codewords, each codeword has an index, and PQ stores the indices of the 
    selected closest codewords instead of storing the original sub-vectors / vector.
    ex:
    Original document chunk
        │
        ▼
	Original embedding
	[0.2, 0.7, ... many numbers ...]
        │
        ▼  PQ indices
	[17, 42]     ← 	compact stored codeword indices 
			Number of PQ indices
        			=
			Number of sub-vectors
    this above done for all chunks of a doc.
    During retrieval:
    User query
   	│
   	▼
	Query embedding
   	│
   	▼
	Build distance lookup tables
   	│
   	▼
	Stored vector [17, 42]
   	│
   	├── lookup distance for codeword 17
   	└── lookup distance for codeword 42
             │
             ▼
       	approximate similarity score
    Then the vector database does this for many stored vectors:
	Chunk A → [17, 42] → score 0.91
	Chunk B → [ 3, 81] → score 0.42
	Chunk C → [17, 10] → score 0.88
	Chunk D → [55, 22] → score 0.15
    
    Then:
    Top scores
   	│
   	▼
	Chunk A
	Chunk C
	...
   	│
   	▼
	RETRIEVAL of A and/or C chunks.
  - Optimized product quantization (OPQ)

  space and computation optimization by compressing vector data.
3⃣️ Graph indexing
4⃣️ Hashing indexing
  Hash functions to map similar high-dimensional vectors into same Hash buckets.
  Search/compare query vector against  only the vectors in the hash bucket that query hash maps to.



Embedding to vector indexing support:

 | Embedding type    | Typical representation                   | Index possibilities                                 |
 | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
 | Dense             | `[0.12, -0.31, 0.87, ...]`               | FLAT, IVF, HNSW, PQ, SQ, etc.                       |
 | Sparse            | `{term_id: weight, ...}`                 | Sparse/inverted indexes, specialized sparse indexes |
 | Binary            | `01011010...`                            | Binary indexes / Hamming-based search               |
 | Quantized dense   | integers/codes representing dense vector | SQ, PQ, etc.                                        |
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

